## Model rationale

### Hy3 as primary workhorse (routed 2026-08-25)

- **Cost**: $0.14 input / $0.55 output per 1M tokens (~$0.04/task on AA benchmarks)
- **Intelligence**: AA Intelligence Index **42** (#15/107 open weights, above median 27)
- **Speed**: 80 t/s output, 2.90s TTFT — decent for a reasoning model
- **Architecture**: 299B total / 21B active (MoE, 192 experts top-8), Apache 2.0
- **Context**: 256k tokens
- **Why Hy3 over MiMo-V2.5**: MiMo has no AA benchmark scores (not in top 29), Hy3 scores 42. Hy3 is 4× more intelligent for ~4× the cost — the best intelligence-per-dollar outside free models.
- **Why Hy3 over DeepSeek V4 Flash**: Flash scores 52 (10 points higher) but costs 3× more ($0.11 vs $0.04/task). For volume agents, the 10-point gap doesn't justify 3× the cost.
- **Fallback chain**: hy3 ($0.04) → gpt-5.6-luna ($0.05) → mimo-v2.5-free ($0, trains)
- **Data training**: paid tier = zero-retention. Free variant (hy3-free) trains on data.

### DeepSeek V4 Flash — quality fallback only

- AA Intelligence Index **52** (#5/107), fastest model (125 t/s, 1.05s TTFT)
- $0.44/$1.32 per 1M tokens — expensive for volume use but justified for quality-critical agents
- Peak/off-peak pricing: peak hours (01:00-04:00, 06:00-10:00 UTC Mon-Fri) = 2× prices
- 7,600 req/5h at $30/mo budget cap
- Used only on oracle/prometheus when GLM-5.2 quota is exhausted, and on test-writer/review
- **No free variant exists** — `deepseek-v4-flash-free` does not exist on the platform

### GLM-5.2 — quality fallback only

- AA Intelligence Index **53** (#4/107), highest intelligence but expensive
- $1.40/$4.40 per 1M tokens, 880 req/5h
- Sits as fallback on reasoning/planning agents (oracle, prometheus, metis, momus, ultrabrain)
- Not primary — 35× more expensive per task than Hy3

### GPT 5.6 Luna — budget fallback

- AA Intelligence Index **52** (tied with Flash), $0.20/$1.20 per 1M tokens
- At max effort: 150s TTFT (always-on CoT, too slow for agents)
- At low effort: 1.61s TTFT, 132 t/s, Intelligence 34 — cheap and fast but weaker
- **Luna is served via OpenAI `/responses`, not chat/completions** — works only through opencode's BUILT-IN provider
- Used as second fallback across all chains

### MiniMax M3 — multimodal only

- AA Intelligence Index **45**, 124 t/s, 1.31s TTFT
- $0.30/$1.20 per 1M tokens, 3,200 req/5h
- Only model supporting image + video input — kept for visual-engineering slot

### MiMo-V2.5 — free fallback only

- No AA benchmark scores (not in top 29 despite being #3 on OpenRouter by usage)
- Paid tier: $0.14/$0.28, 30,100 req/5h — cheap but too weak for primary use
- Free tier: trains on user data, used only as last-resort fallback
- Previously primary (Aug 20-25), demoted after landscape review confirmed weak benchmarks

### Muse Spark 1.2 Contributor — NOT routed

- AA Intelligence Index **57** (#17/187), highest intelligence on the platform
- $0.10/$0.20 on OpenCode Go (10× cheaper than official Meta API $1.25/$4.25)
- **Meta trains on prompts and completions** — the discount is paid with user data
- Sustainable pricing unclear (promotional rate vs official 10× gap)
- User decision: not routed due to data privacy concerns (GitHub identity in repo data)

### Ox Alpha — retired from routing

- Was free with unlimited quota, always-on CoT made it too slow (~3-5s latency overhead)
- Removed from model fields and fallback chains entirely
- If free window ends or model deprecated: fallback auto-catches via Hy3 primary

### fallback_models allowlist (2026-08-02/03, claude-opus-5 incident)

Every agent/category carries an allowlist suppressing OMO's HARDCODED AGENT_MODEL_REQUIREMENTS chain (sisyphus chain[0] was claude-opus-5/anthropic — fired on transient errors despite disabled_providers; see docs/INCIDENTS.md). Pattern: `fallback_models[0]` = primary model, tail = gradient from cheap to expensive.

### Budget layering

Three enforcement layers (deliberate, not a conflict): soft routing target ~$15/mo (falsification trigger above) → hard block via routing + `modelConcurrency` caps (per-model concurrency zeros out budget-blocked models; qwen3.7-max concurrency=0 since 2026-08-02) → monitoring ceiling $60/mo.

## NOT-list (do not do)

1. **NEVER add a custom `opencode-go` provider entry with `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible`** — it breaks gpt-5.6-luna (responses-only transport). The built-in opencode-go provider handles per-model transport.
2. No Kimi K3 ($3/$15), no Grok 4.5 ($2/$6) — spend stays below that tier.
3. No Qwen3.7 Max ($2.50/$7.50) — hallucination-prone (Akita v2 51/C); concurrency 0.
4. No Muse Spark 1.2 Contributor as PRIMARY — Meta trains on prompts/completions. GitHub identity risk. Pricing sustainability unclear (10× below official).
5. Routing scope only — no permission/hook/agent-definition/provider changes.
6. No non-opencode-go / non-deepseek providers.
7. No `opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro` — still the Apr preview; not worth the spend.
8. **Ox Alpha: SKIP** — too slow (always-on CoT latency). Removed from routing entirely.
9. **MiMo-V2.5 paid: SKIP** — no AA benchmark scores, too weak for primary use. Free tier only as last-resort fallback.

## Refresh trigger

Lightweight periodic re-review (LANDSCAPE-lite): when the user senses the
landscape shifted, or a model is added/retired on opencode.ai/docs/go, or
falsification signals appear (frontier outputs no measurable gain ~2 weeks, main
agent 429-starvation, spend >$15/mo). Re-verify against the live model list
(https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/models) and models.dev before changing anything.

## Benchmark integrity (2026-08-03)

When comparing models during LANDSCAPE checks, cite ONLY contamination-resistant
benches. **SWE-bench Verified is DEAD as a signal** — OpenAI Frontier Evals
stopped reporting it (Apr 2026): audit found >60% of tasks unsolvable-as-written,
models reproduce gold patches VERBATIM from task ID, saturated at 93.9%;
any score >80% warrants harness/tool-access scrutiny. Do NOT cite it.

Trustworthy: SWE-bench Pro (contamination-resistant by design — GPL-copyleft
+ private repos, held-out/commercial subsets; frontier ~59% on Pro vs 80-95%
on Verified), LiveCodeBench (time-split, contamination-free by design), HLE +
PaperBench (novel/fail-safe design). Caveat-only: Terminal-Bench 2.1 (canary
"largely symbolic safeguard" per its own paper; interactive-CLI harder to game
but vendor-reported numbers like flash 0731's 82.7 need independent confirm),
BenchLM (aggregate), NL2Repo/Cybergym/Toolathlon/DSBench/DeepSWE (vendor-reported).
